<!--
    A cloned return block wrote a global a second time (kuna #1355).  The
    bytes are decompiler/crates/kuna-cli/tests/fixtures/return_copy_clone.S
    linked at 0x1000.  audit_select is clang 14 -O1 -fPIC:

        audit_recorded = audit_decoy;
        if (audit_choice) return audit_fast;
        return audit_safe;

    It stores audit_recorded once, then picks the result with a CMOVE and
    returns.  SLEIGH lifts the CMOVE as a branch around one move, so the RET
    is a block with two in-edges, and the return splitters (returndup,
    earlyreturn, switchreturn, upstream ActionReturnSplit) clone it per edge.
    Heritage puts an `audit_recorded = audit_recorded` guard COPY before
    every RETURN and flags it return_copy, which keeps the propagation rules
    off it.  The clone dropped that flag, so the stored value was propagated
    into the copy and it printed `audit_recorded = audit_decoy;` again on the
    audit_safe path (#3, #4).

    audit_retry is gcc 11 -O0 -fPIC:

        if (a) { audit_count = 1; if (b) return 1; audit_tick(); return 2; }
        return 3;

    All three paths jump to one `leave; ret`, which holds a phi for
    audit_count.  Splitting turned that phi into `audit_count = audit_count`
    in each copy (and in the original once one edge was left), and
    propagation turned it into a second `audit_count = 1;` on the b path
    (#6, #7).  A cloned guard now keeps its flag, and a phi on address-tied
    storage leaves no copy behind: its readers take the incoming Varnode.
    Both passes run, with returndup (the harness default, #1) and with
    earlyreturn (#2); #5 checks both results are still returned.

    audit_pair (hand-written, at 0x1100) returns 2 without a store, or
    stores its argument to audit_lo and then audit_hi, the next word, and
    returns 1.  Both paths reach one RET, the storing one last.  Copy
    propagation folds the stored register into the RET block's phis and the
    two stores die, so the split prints them from the clone's copies of the
    phis, in the order the phis are cloned, which the binary does not record.
    `option clonephiorder` picks that order: on (the default) clones the phis
    on globals in address order, which is the binary's order here (#8); off
    clones them in heritage's order, the reverse (#9).

    Stage: P8 structure (return-block node split).  #1-#7 are a strict fix,
    no option; #8-#9 pin `option clonephiorder on|off`.
-->
